Here is the exact drop-in replacement to swap out the native TextField for the MUI X DateTimePicker in your override scheduling section.
Since your existing API submission logic likely expects finalScheduleTime to be a string (like "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm"), I have mapped the dayjs object back to a standard format in the onChange handler so you don't have to rewrite your backend submit function.
### 1. Replace the block inside your universal action screen
Find the scheduleOverrideType === "OVERRIDE" block inside your VoucherAuthorization component and update it to this:
```jsx
{scheduleOverrideType === "OVERRIDE" && (
  <Box
    sx={{
      mt: 2,
      mb: 2,
      p: 3, // Slightly more padding to accommodate the helper text
      borderRadius: 2,
      border: "1px solid",
      borderColor: "divider",
      animation: "fadeIn 0.2s ease-in-out",
      backgroundColor: "white",
    }}
  >
    <DateTimePicker
      label="New Execution Date & Time"
      // Convert your existing string state into a dayjs object for the picker
      value={finalScheduleTime ? dayjs(finalScheduleTime) : null}
      onChange={(newValue) => {
        // Convert the dayjs object back to your expected string format on selection
        setFinalScheduleTime(newValue ? newValue.format("YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm") : "");
      }}
      minDateTime={dayjs().add(30, 'minute')}
      maxDateTime={dayjs().add(24, 'hour')}
      timeSteps={{ minutes: 1 }}
      slotProps={{
        textField: {
          size: 'small',
          fullWidth: true,
          required: true,
          sx: { caretColor: 'transparent' },
          helperText: "Scheduling is only allowed between 30 minutes and 24 hours from current time.",
          onKeyDown: (e) => e.preventDefault(),
          onPaste: (e) => e.preventDefault(),
          inputProps: { readOnly: true } // Forces users to use the picker, preventing invalid manual typing
        }
      }}
    />
  </Box>
)}

```
### 2. Make sure you have the required imports
At the top of your file, ensure you have imported dayjs and the DateTimePicker. Your root component or this specific file must also be wrapped in MUI's LocalizationProvider for the picker to work.
```javascript
import dayjs from "dayjs";
import { DateTimePicker } from "@mui/x-date-pickers/DateTimePicker";
// Ensure you have LocalizationProvider wrapping your app or this specific component tree

```
This will give you the polished, highly controlled calendar pop-up while perfectly locking down manual text entry to prevent validation errors.
